Other than "Make your subject specific", no. I don't believe that
there's a real consensus on the necessity of changing subjects in
response to drift (on the other hand, few people would consider it
wrong to do so).

> Also, and this one is pretty specific to rgrn and can't properly fall
> under the heading "Netiquette" at all, but what about spoilers?  A few
> people are meticulous about marking their spoilers and even leaving
> spoiler space.  Others (myself included) just dive right in with all
> sorts of detailed information about the mid and late game.  Personally,
> I can't see the point in reading rgrn if one is trying to remain
> unspoiled.  How many articles would remain if you took out all the
> spoilers?  Only the flames, I suspect.  :-)  So, is there a policy?

  2.2Q:   What's a "spoiler"?

  2.2A:

  A "spoiler" is an explanation of something in the game. That
  explanation may spoil the fun of discovering that something for
  yourself. Since some people would rather figure things out for
  themselves, they don't want the answer blurted out. For this reason,
  if you do post a spoiler, you may wish to buffer it with some blank
  space (20 lines or so, or a formfeed character) so no one reads it
  accidentally. Alternatively, and (given the wide variety of newsreader
  displays) preferably, you can ROT13 encipher the spoiler (a simple
  cipher where a->n, b->o, ... z->m; see <http://www.rot13.com/> if your
  newsreader doesn't support it natively). If you're starting a
  spoilersome thread, consider putting "(spoilers)" in the Subject
  header.

  However, if you read this newsgroup you should accept that you're
  probably going to come across spoilers whether you want to or not.

There's a lot of "may wish to" and "consider" in there. In practice,
spoiler obfuscation tends to be restricted to threads where the
original poster has specifically asked not to be spoiled; in other
cases, it tends to be a judgement call on the part of the responder
whether hints or outright spoilers are appropriate to the OP's
apparent level of experience.

